WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.878
This is lesson one.

00:00:01.878 --> 00:00:06.347
Before a network can learn to walk, we need to be exact about the task.

00:00:06.347 --> 00:00:11.230
What can the walker sense, what can it do, and what counts as doing well?

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.269
Meet the walker.

00:00:13.269 --> 00:00:18.366
It's a box-shaped body on two legs, in a small two dimensional physics world.

00:00:18.366 --> 00:00:22.683
Each leg has a hip and a knee, and every joint has a motor.

00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:25.791
The ground is a bumpy grass track.

00:00:25.791 --> 00:00:30.747
It's generated from a random seed, so every episode gets a different track.

00:00:30.747 --> 00:00:35.704
The goal is to reach the finish, about eighty four units to the right.

00:00:36.304 --> 00:00:39.276
The world runs at fifty steps per second.

00:00:39.276 --> 00:00:46.859
An episode ends when the body touches the ground, when the walker reaches the finish, or after sixteen hundred steps.

00:00:46.859 --> 00:00:49.221
That's thirty two seconds.

00:00:49.821 --> 00:00:53.356
At every step, the walker gets twenty four numbers.

00:00:53.356 --> 00:00:55.273
It never sees a picture.

00:00:55.273 --> 00:01:02.163
Four numbers describe the body: its tilt, how fast it's turning, and how fast it moves forward and up.

00:01:02.763 --> 00:01:04.862
Ten numbers describe the legs.

00:01:04.862 --> 00:01:11.736
For each leg, there's the angle and speed of the hip and the knee, and whether the foot is touching the ground.

00:01:12.336 --> 00:01:14.512
The last ten come from a lidar.

00:01:14.512 --> 00:01:20.816
It's a fan of rays that measures how far away the ground is, from straight down to almost straight ahead.

00:01:20.816 --> 00:01:24.966
That's how the walker can notice a bump before it gets there.

00:01:25.566 --> 00:01:31.115
What it can do is just as simple: four numbers between minus one and one, one per joint.

00:01:31.115 --> 00:01:34.005
The sign says which way to turn the joint.

00:01:34.005 --> 00:01:36.259
The size says how hard to push.

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:38.640
That's the whole controller.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.494
Now the score.

00:01:40.494 --> 00:01:42.547
Moving forward earns reward.

00:01:42.547 --> 00:01:47.351
Crossing the whole track is worth about three hundred and sixty points.

00:01:47.951 --> 00:01:52.812
Every bit of motor effort costs a little, and so does tilting the body.

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:57.603
A good walker spends thirty to sixty points on effort over a run.

00:01:58.203 --> 00:02:00.295
And falling is expensive.

00:02:00.295 --> 00:02:06.679
If the body hits the ground, the walker loses a hundred points, and the episode is over.

00:02:07.279 --> 00:02:12.458
So a walker that reaches the finish without falling scores about three hundred.

00:02:12.458 --> 00:02:17.401
Averaged over a hundred random tracks, three hundred counts as solved.

00:02:18.001 --> 00:02:20.280
There's a hidden catch: time.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:27.601
Eighty four units in sixteen hundred steps means an average speed of about two point six units per second.

00:02:28.201 --> 00:02:32.506
My previous walker, evolved with a method called NEAT, rarely fell.

00:02:32.506 --> 00:02:34.113
But it was too slow.

00:02:34.113 --> 00:02:39.221
It ran out of time at about seventy four units and scored about one seventy five.

00:02:39.221 --> 00:02:44.227
To reach three hundred, a walker has to be safe and fast at the same time.

00:02:44.827 --> 00:02:53.610
So the job is a function from twenty four numbers to four numbers that earns as much reward as possible, on tracks it has never seen.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:58.894
In lesson two, we look at what that function is: a small neural network.
